---
description: Add an Obsidian command following this plugin's patterns
---

Add a new Obsidian command to `src/commands.ts`.

Follow the existing patterns exactly:

- Register through `plugin.commands` (the CommandsService), NOT `this.addCommand`.
  Use the matching helper for the command shape:
  - `addSimple(id, name, callback)` — always available.
  - `addChecked(id, name, when, run)` — conditional (`when` returns `false` to hide; `run` performs it).
  - `addEditor(id, name, editorCallback)` — needs the active editor.
- Use a **bare** command id (no plugin-id prefix — Obsidian adds it).
- Never set a default hotkey.
- All user-facing text (command name, notices, modal copy) goes through `t(...)`
  with a new key added to `src/i18n/en.json` — literal strings fail the i18n
  lint rule.
- User feedback goes through `plugin.notices` / `plugin.modals`, never a raw
  `new Notice(...)`.

Then add a unit test in `tests/unit/commands.test.ts` asserting the command is
registered (assert against the obsidian mock's recorded commands), and run
`{{run}} typecheck && {{run}} lint && {{run}} test` before finishing.

$ARGUMENTS
